(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which condition belongs in the differential of subacute cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Acute aortic dissection","Massive pulmonary embolism","Right ventricular infarction","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Subacute cardiac tamponade is mimicked by constrictive pericarditis, congestive heart failure and cirrhosis. Acute tamponade has its own differential: myocardial infarction (especially with right ventricular involvement), aortic dissection and massive pulmonary embolism. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the Kussmaul sign?","choices":["Blood pressure drops 10 mmHg on inspiration","Jugular pressure falls on inspiration","QRS amplitude alternates beat to beat","Jugular pressure rises on inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In constrictive pericarditis the jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign: it increases during inspiration rather than decreasing. It sits alongside the pericardial knock and, uncommonly, pericardial calcification among the findings of constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Pericarditis that develops after a myocardial infarction or cardiac surgery carries which eponym?","choices":["Spodick sign","Dressler syndrome","Beck triad","Kussmaul sign"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis after myocardial infarction or after cardiotomy, such as cardiac surgery, carries the eponym Dressler syndrome. It is one of the listed etiologies of pericardial disease, alongside idiopathic and viral disease, malignancy, radiation, autoimmune disorders, uremia, tuberculosis and medications. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Before committing to pericardiectomy, which test helps identify inflammatory, potentially reversible constriction?","choices":["Transesophageal echocardiogram","Chest radiograph for calcium","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Exercise stress electrocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance showing edema or late gadolinium enhancement helps identify inflammatory, potentially reversible constriction before committing to pericardiectomy. It is now also recommended for risk stratification of complicated or recurrent pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which maneuver increases the intensity of a pericardial friction rub?","choices":["Leaning forward while holding a breath","Lying flat on the left side","Squatting from a standing position","Light pressure with the stethoscope bell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pericardial friction rub increases in intensity with the patient leaning forward, holding a breath, and with firm pressure of the stethoscope diaphragm. It is classically triphasic, scratchy or squeaking, loudest at the left sternal border, and highly specific for acute pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which imaging helps evaluate pericardial thickness when constriction is suspected but unconfirmed?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan or plain radiograph","Exercise treadmill test or stress echocardiogram","Abdominal ultrasound and liver elastography","Cardiac magnetic resonance or computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance or computed tomography helps evaluate pericardial thickness, especially when constriction is suspected but unconfirmed. Magnetic resonance can also show acute inflammation, while echocardiography remains the test that confirms constrictive physiology in many patients. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"About what proportion of patients with acute pericarditis go on to recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["1 to 5%","15 to 30%","50 to 60%","Over 80%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients with acute pericarditis. By comparison, constriction is estimated to follow in under 0.05% of patients with viral pericarditis, so recurrence is the common complication and constriction the rare one. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which jugular venous waveform pattern is described in cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Absent x descent, prominent y descent","Giant cannon a waves on every beat","Large v waves with each systole","Prominent x descent, absent y descent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade the jugular venous waveform shows a prominent x descent with an absent y descent. It accompanies a greatly elevated jugular venous pressure and pulsus paradoxus in a heart whose diastolic filling is mechanically impaired by the effusion. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How should all large pericardial effusions be followed?","choices":["With serial transthoracic echocardiograms","With monthly electrocardiograms","With repeat cardiac catheterization","With serial C-reactive protein alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All large pericardial effusions should be followed with serial transthoracic echocardiograms, the gold-standard test that shows the effusion and any right ventricular diastolic collapse. Imaging is paired with close clinical follow-up from the specialty matching the cause, such as cardiology, infectious disease, nephrology, oncology or rheumatology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"In the approach that uses C-reactive protein to guide therapy, when does tapering begin?","choices":["Once symptom-free with a normal C-reactive protein","Once the friction rub can no longer be heard","After the electrocardiogram returns to normal","Once C-reactive protein starts to fall, despite pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the approach that uses C-reactive protein, the level is checked at presentation and then weekly, and tapering starts when the patient is symptom-free and the C-reactive protein has normalized. The other approach starts tapering once the patient has been symptom-free for at least 24 hours. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drugs are the anti-interleukin-1 agents used for recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Rituximab and tocilizumab","Azathioprine and colchicine","Anakinra and rilonacept","Infliximab and adalimumab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anakinra and rilonacept are the anti-interleukin-1 agents, preferred for recurrent or colchicine-resistant disease or steroid-dependent disease. The 2025 guidelines support using them earlier with an inflammatory phenotype: C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever or evidence of pericardial inflammation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition is a key mimic in the differential of constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Isolated aortic stenosis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Acute myocardial infarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The differential of constrictive pericarditis is subacute cardiac tamponade, restrictive cardiomyopathy and cirrhosis, and restrictive cardiomyopathy is the cardiac mimic among them. The initial workup includes B-type natriuretic peptide, and echocardiographic features such as pericardial thickening and septal bounce support constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Pericarditis symptoms persisting beyond which point earn the label chronic pericarditis?","choices":["2 weeks","6 weeks","3 months","2 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic pericarditis is the label when symptoms persist for more than 3 months. Acute pericarditis lasts under 4 to 6 weeks, and incessant pericarditis covers persistence beyond 4 to 6 weeks up to 3 months without a sustained remission. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph appearance suggests a large pericardial effusion?","choices":["Globular, water-bottle cardiac silhouette","Boot-shaped heart with an upturned apex","Egg-on-a-string cardiac silhouette","Bat-wing perihilar opacities"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A large effusion shows on chest radiograph as cardiomegaly, an enlarged cardiac silhouette with a globular, water-bottle shape, and clear lung fields may be present. At least 200 mL of pericardial fluid must accumulate before the silhouette enlarges. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What is the definitive treatment for established constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Pericardiocentesis","Lifelong colchicine","Cardiac transplantation","Pericardiectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis is often a surgical disease, and pericardiectomy, surgical removal of part or most of the pericardium, is its definitive treatment. Pericardiectomy increases 5-year survival to nearly 80% and 10-year survival to nearly 60%, and earlier intervention improves outcomes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which is a recognized cause of cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Free-wall rupture after infarction","Uncomplicated mitral valve prolapse","Well-controlled essential hypertension","Stable exertional angina pectoris"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Free-wall rupture after myocardial infarction is a listed cause of tamponade, alongside acute pericarditis, iatrogenic injury, penetrating trauma, malignancy and aortic dissection. A rupture fills the pericardium quickly, and rapidly growing effusions cause tamponade early because the pericardium cannot stretch. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"In the symptom-based approach, tapering starts once the patient has been symptom-free for at least how long?","choices":["1 hour","24 hours","7 days","4 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When treatment duration is based on symptom resolution alone, tapering starts once the patient has been symptom-free for at least 24 hours. The alternative approach also requires a normalized C-reactive protein, checked at presentation and then weekly. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which pericardial fluid studies are sent when tuberculosis is suspected?","choices":["Gram stain and bacterial culture","Antinuclear antibody on the fluid","Acid-fast stain and mycobacterial culture","Cytology to look for malignant cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When tuberculous pericarditis is suspected, pericardial fluid is sent for an acid-fast bacilli stain and mycobacterial culture. These sit with the other fluid studies (cell count, Gram stain, bacterial and fungal culture, and cytology), while a purified protein derivative skin test belongs to the broader effusion workup. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Pericarditis symptoms that persist beyond 4 to 6 weeks, up to 3 months, without a sustained remission define which form?","choices":["Incessant pericarditis","Recurrent pericarditis","Chronic pericarditis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Incessant pericarditis is defined by symptoms that persist for more than 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission. Beyond 3 months the course is labeled chronic, and a return after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer is recurrent. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Failure to respond to a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine within 1 week suggests what?","choices":["Normal recovery that simply needs time","An allergy to the colchicine","Constriction that was missed at first","A cause other than idiopathic or viral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Failure to respond to a nonsteroidal drug plus colchicine within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral pericarditis. Treatment failure is defined by persistent fever or pleuritic chest pain, a new pericardial effusion or worsening general illness. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which tests screen for nephrotic syndrome as a cause of pericardial effusion?","choices":["Albumin and urine protein","Thyroid function tests","Tuberculin skin test","Blood cultures and lactate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and urine protein are the tests in the effusion workup that screen for nephrotic syndrome. They sit beside thyroid function tests for myxedema, a rheumatologic workup, infectious testing (human immunodeficiency virus, Lyme serology, tuberculin skin test) and a malignancy evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the median life expectancy with a symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion?","choices":["2 to 4 weeks","2 to 4 months","2 to 4 years","About 10 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy, a median of 2 to 4 months, and recurrent effusion often develops within that time. A palliative care consult is recommended to improve clinical outcomes and quality of life. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which study is occasionally needed to confirm constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Right heart catheterization with angiography","Endomyocardial biopsy of the septum","Transesophageal echo with bubble study","Serial troponin over twenty-four hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coronary angiography with right heart catheterization is occasionally needed to confirm constrictive pericarditis. It comes after echocardiography, which confirms constrictive physiology in many patients, and after cardiac magnetic resonance or computed tomography when the diagnosis is suspected but unconfirmed. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of corticosteroids is a particular concern for older adults with pericarditis?","choices":["Hypoglycemia","Osteoporosis","Bradycardia","Hearing loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults with pericarditis, corticosteroids raise osteoporosis risk, which makes them a particular concern in this group. That sits beside the other age-specific cautions: anti-inflammatory drugs on the Beers list and colchicine considered for dose reduction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"If pericardiectomy is planned, how should diuretics be used beforehand?","choices":["Stop them completely","Intravenously instead","Sparingly","Aggressively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If pericardiectomy is planned, diuretics should be used sparingly in the preoperative period. Diuretics still have a role for fluid overload in constriction, including the medical trial for early, hemodynamically stable disease, which is why the advice is restraint rather than avoidance. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which situation calls for referral to cardiology in acute pericarditis?","choices":["A first, uncomplicated episode","Pain that eases after one dose","A typical viral prodrome","A recurrence after recovery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Referral to cardiology is advised for recurrences (recurrent pericarditis), for no response to conservative management and for development of constrictive pericarditis. A recurrence after recovery meets the first of those triggers. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding is fairly specific for cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Left axis deviation","Electrical alternans","Diffuse PR depression","Peaked T waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Electrical alternans, in which the QRS amplitude alternates from beat to beat, is fairly specific for cardiac tamponade, although it can rarely appear with very large effusions. It typically comes with low-voltage QRS complexes and sinus tachycardia. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which patient should be considered for diagnostic pericardiocentesis?","choices":["Typical first episode of pericarditis","Any effusion found on a routine scan","Moderate-to-large effusion of unknown cause","Small effusion after a viral illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic pericardiocentesis is considered in patients undergoing therapeutic drainage for tamponade, with high suspicion for neoplastic, purulent or tuberculous pericarditis, or with a moderate-to-large effusion of unknown etiology. The last describes exactly this patient group. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which feature predicts a poorer prognosis in acute pericarditis?","choices":["Prompt response within a week","Subacute onset over days to weeks","Pain eased by sitting forward","A preceding viral illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A subacute onset is among the features linked to the poorest prognosis, seen in about 15% of patients. The others are fever above 38 degrees Celsius, a large effusion with or without tamponade, lack of response to anti-inflammatory drugs after 1 week, myopericarditis, traumatic pericarditis and oral anticoagulation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which bacterial cause of pericarditis is linked to rheumatic pancarditis?","choices":["Mycoplasma","Histoplasma","Streptococcus","Staphylococcus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among the bacterial causes of pericarditis, Streptococcus is the one tied to rheumatic pancarditis. The other bacterial causes listed are Staphylococcus, Pneumococcus, Haemophilus, Mycoplasma and Mycobacterium tuberculosis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"}]};
